WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.360
How do we know proteins really travel in this order?

2
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.400
The first film drew the route, from the rough ER to the Golgi to the cell membrane.

3
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.160
But a drawing is not evidence.

4
00:00:16.283 --> 00:00:21.463
In 1967, James Jamieson and George Palade found a way to test it.

5
00:00:22.663 --> 00:00:25.243
They used thin slices of guinea pig pancreas.

6
00:00:26.403 --> 00:00:30.023
The cells they studied make digestive enzymes, not insulin.

7
00:00:31.303 --> 00:00:33.003
But they too send proteins out.

8
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:36.137
Here is the problem.

9
00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:39.397
A cell is always making new protein.

10
00:00:40.597 --> 00:00:45.397
If every protein carries a mark, the mark is everywhere, and you learn nothing about

11
00:00:45.397 --> 00:00:48.577
order. You need to mark just one batch.

12
00:00:49.617 --> 00:00:54.057
So they gave the slices a short pulse of a radioactive building block.

13
00:00:55.317 --> 00:00:58.257
Only proteins made during those few minutes carried the mark.

14
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.840
Then came the chase: plenty of the ordinary building block.

15
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.340
Proteins made after that carried no mark.

16
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.360
The marked batch was now on its own.

17
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:12.313
They followed the batch in two ways.

18
00:01:13.553 --> 00:01:18.493
In one, broken cells were spun in a centrifuge, so their parts settled into layers.

19
00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:24.633
In the other, electron microscope pictures showed where radioactive grains sat.

20
00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:27.097
Time for a prediction.

21
00:01:28.517 --> 00:01:30.297
The clock reads zero minutes of chase.

22
00:01:31.597 --> 00:01:35.717
Right after the pulse, where do you predict most of the marked protein will be?

23
00:01:42.183 --> 00:01:43.403
Over the rough ER.

24
00:01:44.583 --> 00:01:47.283
At the start of the chase, most grains sat there.

25
00:01:48.443 --> 00:01:50.503
That is where ribosomes build new protein.

26
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.367
Minutes later, the marked batch had spread out.

27
00:01:55.627 --> 00:02:00.207
By seven minutes, Golgi vesicles held almost as many grains as the rough ER.

28
00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:04.667
Golgi vesicles are small vesicles at the Golgi's edge.

29
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.210
Later still, the mark moved on again.

30
00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.950
The grains gathered in condensing vacuoles.

31
00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.990
These are storage granules that are still being formed.

32
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:21.450
At the last time point shown, more than half the grains sat in finished storage

33
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:26.290
granules. These hold the protein until the cell releases it.

34
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:29.663
Each part's share of the mark peaked in turn.

35
00:02:31.243 --> 00:02:36.483
Rough ER, then Golgi vesicles, then condensing vacuoles, then storage granules.

36
00:02:37.603 --> 00:02:40.123
The chart shows only four moments of that journey.

37
00:02:41.217 --> 00:02:44.637
These were digestive enzyme cells, not beta cells.

38
00:02:46.037 --> 00:02:50.597
Still, this experiment gave direct evidence for the order of stops that textbooks

39
00:02:50.597 --> 00:02:54.137
still draw. A drawing had become a measurement.

40
00:02:55.217 --> 00:02:56.797
So how do we know the order?

41
00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:01.837
Mark one batch of new protein, then watch where it goes over time.

42
00:03:03.157 --> 00:03:05.757
Each stop's share peaks later than the stop before it.
